course: dictionary
lesson: 5408
title: Label: a word with two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A label is a small piece of paper, cloth or plastic stuck on something to give information about it. To label something means to put a label on it, or to write names on its parts. «A message is cut into many small packets, each labelled with the address it must reach.» Each packet carries a label that says where it must go.
explain: In science and maths you label diagrams: you write the names of the parts and draw lines pointing to them. You also label the axes on a graph, so everyone knows what the numbers stand for. To check your meaning, swap in a word like tag or name and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Label can also be a verb about people. To label someone is to describe them with a word, often unfairly. The words around it tell you which meaning is needed: a label on a jar is a thing, but to label a person is to judge them.
fact: A label is a noun: a small piece of paper, cloth or plastic with information on it.
fact: To label is a verb: to put a label on something, or to write names on its parts.
fact: Food labels can show allergies and sell-by dates, and medicine labels show how much to take.
fact: «Taking more of a medicine than the dose on the label can poison you.» Always read the label first.
fact: Labelled (British spelling) and labeled (American spelling) are both correct.
exercise: «A message is cut into many small packets, each labelled with the address it must reach.» What does labelled mean here? | *Given a tag with information on it | Cut into smaller pieces | Wrapped in a warm blanket | Sent without any address
exercise: Ella must label the parts of a plant on her diagram. What does she do? | Draws a picture of the whole plant in colour | *Writes the names of the parts, with lines pointing to them | Counts the leaves | Cuts the plant into pieces
exercise: «Wax crayons are wrapped in paper labels by machine.» Which of these is the label? | The wax that melts when it is hot | The crayon itself | *The paper sticker that gives information about the crayons | The machine
test: Sam says, «Don't label him as lazy just because he is quiet.» What does label mean here? | To write his name on a diagram | *To describe someone with a word, often unfairly | To stick a paper tag on him | To give him a prize
test: Which is the best check for the meaning of label in a sentence? | Look only at the first letter | Count the letters in the word | *Swap in a word like tag or name and see if it still makes sense | Skip it and never read the text again
exam: Mia reads, «Taking more of a medicine than the dose on the label can poison you.» Why should she read the label before taking medicine? | It tells her to take extra | It tells her the colour of the medicine | It tells her the time of the bus | *It tells her how much to take
